Estimating riverbed topography from flow velocity data is a complex geostatistical issue. 
Recently, a combination of principal component analysis and geostatistical methods (PCGA) was proposed to address this problem. 
Although the PCGA method is effective, it is not straightforward to apply and comprehending its performance requires an in-depth analysis. 
To achieve this, a comprehensive synthetic dataset with a comprehensive range of scenarios was generated to facilitate a rigorous evaluation of the PCGA procedure. 
The results indicate that the PCGA method's accuracy is influenced by several factors, including: 
- using an inadequate number of principal components to represent velocity data; 
- employing an insufficient number of principal component realizations in the Monte Carlo simulation; 
- the impact of measurement errors and symbolic contamination of flow data; 
- the impact of errors and data contamination on the prediction of the riverbed profile; 
- the impact of changes in riverbed topography; and 
- the impact of flow data that does not conform to a Gaussian distribution. 
The findings of this study can aid in designing future field experiments and real-world applications.
